time-services for Debian
========================

System user and RTC ownership
-----------------------------

The daemon does not run as root.  Installing this package creates a system
user and group named "rtc" (via /usr/lib/sysusers.d/time-services.conf) and
installs a udev rule that hands /dev/rtc0 to that group:

  ACTION=="add", KERNEL=="rtc0", SUBSYSTEM=="rtc", OWNER="rtc", GROUP="rtc", MODE="0640"

The mode is deliberately read-only for the group: time_daemon only issues
RTC_RD_TIME, never RTC_SET_TIME.  It adjusts the system clock with
settimeofday(2), for which the unit grants it CAP_SYS_TIME and nothing more.

Because the udev rule only fires on "add", /dev/rtc0 keeps its previous
ownership until the next reboot or until the rule is replayed:

  udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=rtc --action=add

State
-----

Time offsets are persisted in /var/lib/time, created by the service unit's
StateDirectory= and owned by the rtc user.  The path is compiled into the
daemon (OFFSET_LOCATION in time_genoff.h) and is not configurable.

Hardware requirements
---------------------

time_daemon talks to the modem over QMI and is only useful on Qualcomm
platforms with a modem reachable through libqmi-framework.  On other
hardware the service will start and fail to reach the QMI time service.

